 "verdict": "8 clips sourced, fetched, prepared and aligned. All 8 pass the geometry/frame-correspondence/colour/shuffle alignment proof at h264_high (0/8 problems). At h264_typical 1/8 clips (announcement-another-step-toward-one-of, a near-static podium speaker) drops its off-by-one alignment margin below the tool's 1.0 dB bar (0.75 dB); at h264_poor 2/8 clips (that same announcement clip at 0.19 dB and speech-artemis-ii-live-launch-coverage-c at 0.65 dB) drop below it. In every case the shuffle-collapse control stays strongly positive (>=6.9 dB), proving temporal correspondence is still real; the low margin is a low-motion artifact (near-static talking-head footage compresses adjacent frames to be very similar), not a data or geometry defect. The other 6 clips - all with real head/hand/crowd motion - keep >=1.8 dB margins even at h264_poor. Geometry (2560x1440 master / 1280x720 decoded, exact frame counts, zero duplicate master frames), colour (luma slope 0.99-1.00, intercept <=0.004 in all 24 cells) and the >=2560x1440 verified active picture area are clean across all 8 clips and all 3 conditions. Faces CAN now be measured validly: every clip's ground truth is a real optical crop, never a synthetic upscale, and the alignment proof is honest end to end."
